{"text": "BRONX BOY SLAIN; ZIP GUN IS SOUGHT: Conference Earlier in the Day ...\nNew York Times (1923-Current file); Dec 20, 1953;\nProQuest Historical Newspapers: The New York Times (1851-2009)\n\npg. 28\n\nBRONX BOY SLAIN; |\nZIP GUN 1S SOUGHT\n\nConference Earlier in the Day\nDiscusses Ways to Combat\nUse of Homemade Pistol |\n\nA 15-year-old Bronx boy was/\nfatally wounded last night by\nwhat the police believe was a \u201czip\ngun.\u201d Only a few hours earlier zip\nSuns, which are homemade weap-|\nlons, had been the subject of a spe-\ncial conference on ways to combat!\nthem.\nThe youth, Jerry Piro of 703\nEast 187th Street, died at Ford-\nham Hospital about four hours\nafter he was found at 8:23 P. M.\nlying in a hallway at 701 East\n180th Street, seven blocks from his\nhome, with a bullet wound in his\nskuli.\nIn the basement of the building\nthe police found a clubroom ap-\n\n\u2018parently belonging to a youth\ngang known as the Imperial\nHoods and containing at least six)\nzip guns.\nThe Piro boy was believed to\nhave been the victim of a juvenile\ngang war, and at least fifteen\nyouths were seized by the police\nfor questioning in the slaying.\nMethods of combating the \u201czip-\nSun evil\u201d were discussed yesterday\nlafternoon by sportsmen and State;\n\u2018Assembiymen Thomas A. Dwyer\nand Frank J. Pino of Brooklyn.\nThe zip gun, or homemade pistol\nused by teen-aged delinquents, was\nthe subject of a conference at the\nAstor Hotel of representatives of\nthe National Rifle Association of\nAmerica, the Associated Sports-\njmen\u2019s Council on Firearms, the\n\u2018New York State Conservation\nCouncil and court authorities.\n|\" \u2018They recommended a re-exami-\nnation and r\u00e9vision of present fire-\narms laws by a joint legislative\ncommittee and a program to edu-\ncate and train youth in proper use\nof firearms.\nThe re-examination of the fire-\narms laws was proposed as part\nof an effort to devise regulations;\n\nthat would eliminate zip guns and\nindiscriminate use of .22-calibre\nammunition without at the same\ntime affecting more responsible\nusers of firearms,\nThose attending the conference\nwere Maj. Gen, Merritt A. Edson,|\n\u2018executive director of the National\nRifle Association; Special Sessions|\n|Sudge John E.\u2019 Cone; | Michael!\nNadel and Michael Petruska of the)\nNew York State Conservation|\nCouncil; Arthur Wagoner of the)\nAssociated Sportsmen\u2019s Council on/|\nFirearms; Mr. Dwyer and Mr.|\nPino; and J. Kenneth McCabe. as-|\nsistant district attorney of Kings:\nCounty. |\n\nReproduced with permission of the copyright owner. Further reproduction prohibited without permission.",
